From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 22:50:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] power: reset: msm-poweroff: outrank PSCI when the firmware
 owns PS_HOLD

With the secure-world deassert wired up, msm-poweroff can actually take the
platform down on msm8998, so it should be the handler that does -- PSCI's
SYSTEM_RESET is a cold reset there and destroys the ramoops region.

Gate the priority on the same cached availability the deassert uses, so the
configuration that was measured broken becomes unreachable by construction:
where the firmware does not answer for PS_HOLD, the handler stays at 128 and
PSCI keeps the job exactly as before. On msm8998 without the SCM call, winning
the arbitration meant the SoC never reset and the hardware watchdog rescued it
-- a 49 s reboot ending in androidboot.bootreason=watchdog.

Also drop the ten-second delay to one. It predates sys_off handler chaining:
returning NOTIFY_DONE lets the next handler try, and a platform that is going
to reset has done so long before a second is up. Ten seconds only guarantees
the watchdog gets there first, which is precisely the failure above.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.c       | 16 +++++++--
 drivers/power/reset/msm-poweroff.c     | 50 +++++++++++++++++++++++---
 include/linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h |  2 +-
 3 files changed, 60 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.c b/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.c
index bc20bb28ce0f..ff73c51be6bf 100644
--- a/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.c
+++ b/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.c
@@ -514,7 +514,15 @@ static int qcom_scm_disable_sdi(void)
 	ret = qcom_scm_clk_enable();
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
-	ret = qcom_scm_call(__scm->dev, &desc, &res);
+
+	/*
+	 * Atomic, not yielding. The vendor issues this one with
+	 * scm_call2_atomic() and mainline's yielding call is refused by at
+	 * least one firmware: on msm8998 it returns status 2 and SDI stays
+	 * enabled, which leaves the board hanging in debug mode on a reboot or
+	 * a watchdog bite rather than resetting.
+	 */
+	ret = qcom_scm_call_atomic(__scm->dev, &desc, &res);
 
 	qcom_scm_clk_disable();
 
@@ -1192,8 +1200,10 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold_available);
  *
  * Takes the platform down. Does not return when the firmware honours it, so
  * the caller must have a fallback for the case where it does.
+ *
+ * Return: never, on success. The firmware's status if it declined.
  */
-void qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold(void)
+int qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold(void)
 {
 	struct qcom_scm_desc desc = {
 		.svc = QCOM_SCM_SVC_PWR,
@@ -1203,7 +1213,7 @@ void qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold(void)
 		.owner = ARM_SMCCC_OWNER_SIP,
 	};
 
-	qcom_scm_call_atomic(__scm->dev, &desc, NULL);
+	return qcom_scm_call_atomic(__scm->dev, &desc, NULL);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold);
 
diff --git a/drivers/power/reset/msm-poweroff.c b/drivers/power/reset/msm-poweroff.c
index 73f05d3604a8..95951c18a4bd 100644
--- a/drivers/power/reset/msm-poweroff.c
+++ b/drivers/power/reset/msm-poweroff.c
@@ -30,11 +30,40 @@ static bool scm_owns_ps_hold;
 
 static int do_msm_poweroff(struct sys_off_data *data)
 {
-	if (scm_owns_ps_hold)
-		qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold();
+	if (scm_owns_ps_hold) {
+		int ret;
+
+		/*
+		 * Stop SPMI traffic before PS_HOLD goes. Lowering it in the
+		 * middle of a transaction locks the PMIC up, and the vendor
+		 * halts the arbiter immediately before every deassert for
+		 * exactly that reason. Measured on msm8998: without this the
+		 * deassert wedges the CPU instead of resetting, and the
+		 * hardware watchdog rescues it ~30 s later.
+		 */
+		qcom_scm_halt_spmi_pmic_arbiter();
+
+		ret = qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold();
+
+		/*
+		 * Only reached when the firmware declined: a honoured deassert
+		 * does not return. Say so, because the fallback below then
+		 * decides whether the platform goes down at all.
+		 */
+		pr_emerg("msm-restart: scm deassert returned %d\n", ret);
+	}
 
 	writel(0, msm_ps_hold);
-	mdelay(10000);
+
+	/*
+	 * Both routes above are asynchronous, so give the platform a moment to
+	 * go down -- but only a moment. The historical ten seconds predate
+	 * sys_off handler chaining: returning NOTIFY_DONE lets the next handler
+	 * (PSCI, at a lower priority) try instead, and a device that is going
+	 * to reset has done so long before a second is up. Waiting the full ten
+	 * only guarantees the hardware watchdog gets there first.
+	 */
+	mdelay(1000);
 
 	return NOTIFY_DONE;
 }
@@ -49,8 +78,21 @@ static int msm_restart_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	dev_info(&pdev->dev, "PS_HOLD deassert: %s\n",
 		 scm_owns_ps_hold ? "secure world" : "register write only");
 
+	/*
+	 * PSCI registers its restart handler at 129. Outranking it is only
+	 * correct where PS_HOLD actually takes the platform down: where the
+	 * bare register write is ignored and the firmware does not implement
+	 * the deassert either, winning the arbitration means the SoC never
+	 * resets and the hardware watchdog rescues it seconds later. Measured
+	 * on msm8998 before the SCM call existed: a 49 s reboot ending in
+	 * bootreason=watchdog.
+	 *
+	 * So take priority only on the firmwares that answer for PS_HOLD, and
+	 * leave everyone else exactly where they were.
+	 */
 	devm_register_sys_off_handler(&pdev->dev, SYS_OFF_MODE_RESTART,
-				      128, do_msm_poweroff, NULL);
+				      scm_owns_ps_hold ? SYS_OFF_PRIO_HIGH : 128,
+				      do_msm_poweroff, NULL);
 
 	devm_register_sys_off_handler(&pdev->dev, SYS_OFF_MODE_POWER_OFF,
 				      SYS_OFF_PRIO_DEFAULT, do_msm_poweroff,
diff --git a/include/linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h b/include/linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h
index 91461d1b78d6..10f802c7cb8f 100644
--- a/include/linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h
+++ b/include/linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h
@@ -98,7 +98,7 @@ int qcom_scm_io_readl(phys_addr_t addr, unsigned int *val);
 int qcom_scm_io_writel(phys_addr_t addr, unsigned int val);
 
 bool qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold_available(void);
-void qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold(void);
+int qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold(void);
 void qcom_scm_halt_spmi_pmic_arbiter(void);
 
 bool qcom_scm_restore_sec_cfg_available(void);
